<div class="wrap">
  <header class="masthead">
    {% if logo %}<img class="logo" src="{{ logo }}" alt="">{% endif %}
    <h1>Gasoline gets the attention. Diesel gets everything else.</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Every mutually exclusive category in the Consumer Price Index, sized by
      its published relative importance and colored by how diesel reaches it.
      Hover any box to read the mechanism.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="scoreboard">
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="num gas">{{ s.gasoline_direct }}%</div>
      <div class="lab"><span class="lab-full">Gasoline, bought directly. One
        line item, and essentially gasoline's whole role in the
        index.</span><span class="lab-terse">Gasoline, bought
        directly</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="num diesel">{{ s.diesel_direct }}%</div>
      <div class="lab"><span class="lab-full">Diesel, bought directly —
        {{ s.gasoline_over_diesel_direct }}× smaller than gasoline. This is the
        number that gets diesel ignored.</span><span class="lab-terse">Diesel,
        bought directly — {{ s.gasoline_over_diesel_direct }}× smaller</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="num diesel">{{ s.exposed_share }}%</div>
      <div class="lab"><span class="lab-full">Of the index sits in a category
        diesel reaches as an input cost — {{ s.reach_ratio }}× gasoline's direct
        weight.</span><span class="lab-terse">Of the index diesel reaches —
        {{ s.reach_ratio }}× gasoline</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="num diesel">{{ s.embedded_diesel }}%</div>
      <div class="lab"><span class="lab-full">Estimated embedded diesel cost —
        {{ s.embedded_vs_gasoline }}× gasoline's entire direct weight, spread
        across {{ s.exposed_count }} categories.</span><span class="lab-terse">Embedded
        diesel cost — {{ s.embedded_vs_gasoline }}× gasoline</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="controls" id="controls"></div>
  <p class="readout" id="readout" aria-live="polite"></p>

  <div class="chartbox" id="chartbox">
    <canvas id="tm" role="img"
      aria-label="Treemap of {{ s.categories }} CPI expenditure categories sized by
      relative importance and colored by diesel exposure. Diesel-exposed
      categories total {{ s.exposed_share }} percent of the index; gasoline bought
      directly is {{ s.gasoline_direct }} percent."></canvas>
    <div class="tip" id="tip"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend" id="legend"></div>

  {# Both shown only in a narrow frame, where the full legend and the stamp are
     dropped to give the treemap back the height they were costing it. The key
     still has to carry the encoding, so it collapses the six-step diesel ramp
     into one strip rather than nine rows. #}
  <div class="keymini" id="keymini"></div>
  <p class="mobile-note">Full legend, footnotes and disclosures on the desktop
    version.</p>

  {# The one credit line that survives the embedded layout, where .notes is
     hidden. Standalone it would only repeat the source paragraph below. #}
  <p class="source">
    Weights: {{ source_publisher }}, relative importance, December {{ ri_year }}.
    Exposure tiers are editorial.
    <a href="{{ publisher_url }}">{{ publisher_name }}</a>
  </p>

  <div class="notes">
    <p><strong>What this does and does not say.</strong> "Diesel reaches
      {{ s.exposed_share }}% of the index" means diesel is an input cost somewhere
      inside categories making up {{ s.exposed_share }}% of the basket. It does
      <em>not</em> mean diesel drives {{ s.exposed_share }}% of inflation. Weighting
      each category by a rough estimate of how much of its delivered cost is
      actually diesel gives an embedded diesel cost of about
      <strong>{{ s.embedded_diesel }}% of the index</strong> — roughly
      {{ s.embedded_vs_gasoline }}× gasoline's entire direct weight, but spread
      across {{ s.exposed_count }} categories instead of sitting in one box. Breadth
      is the claim here; that second number keeps it from being read as
      magnitude.</p>
    <p><strong>Method.</strong> Weights are the published relative importances
      from {{ source_publisher }}, taken at a single depth of the item tree so the
      {{ s.categories }} categories are mutually exclusive and sum to 100% — no
      category contains another. Motor fuel and household energy are split one
      level further so gasoline, diesel and heating oil are visible separately.
      Two rows in the published table carry the wrong indent level and are
      corrected before the tree is built; without that, the cut sums to 101.7%
      rather than 100%.</p>
    <p><strong>The tiers are editorial.</strong> The weights are BLS's. The
      exposure tiers and the narrative on each box are ours — a judgment about
      freight intensity, not a measured cost share, and not a BLS aggregate.
      Every assignment is listed with its reasoning in the project's
      <code>exposure_tiers.py</code> so it can be argued with one category at a
      time. Air travel is fuel-exposed but to jet fuel, and electricity and
      piped gas to their own fuels; both are deliberately excluded from the
      diesel total.</p>
    <p>Source: <a href="{{ source_url }}">{{ source_title }}</a>,
      {{ source_publisher }}.{{ export_note }}</p>
  </div>
</div>

<script>window.PAYLOAD={{ payload }};</script>
<script>{{ js }}</script>
